### Why is an unbounded queue dangerous under overload?

The core insight comes from Little's Law: queue depth = arrival rate x time-in-system. If arrivals exceed the service rate even slightly and persistently, the backlog grows without limit, and latency grows with it.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class LittlesLawDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double arrivalRate = 200;  // requests per second
        double serviceRate = 150;  // requests per second
        double surplus = arrivalRate - serviceRate;

        for (int second = 1; second <= 5; second++) {
            double queueDepth = surplus * second;
            double latencySeconds = queueDepth / serviceRate;
            System.out.printf("After %ds: queue=%,.0f, latency=%.1fs%n",
                    second, queueDepth, latencySeconds);
        }
        System.out.println("Queue and latency grow WITHOUT LIMIT");
    }
}
```

```text
After 1s: queue=50, latency=0.3s
After 2s: queue=100, latency=0.7s
After 3s: queue=150, latency=1.0s
After 4s: queue=200, latency=1.3s
After 5s: queue=250, latency=1.7s
Queue and latency grow WITHOUT LIMIT
```

### Why does the system appear available but silently fail?

The system keeps returning "accepted" to every caller, so health checks pass and dashboards look fine. But queued work ages past usefulness. By the time it is processed, the client has already timed out and retried, so the system burns capacity on stale work.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class SilentFailureDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long clientTimeout = 5000; // client gives up after 5 seconds
        long queueWaitMs = 30_000; // item waits 30 seconds in the queue

        System.out.println("Client timeout: " + clientTimeout + "ms");
        System.out.println("Actual queue wait: " + queueWaitMs + "ms");
        System.out.println("By the time the request is processed:");
        System.out.println("  - Client timed out " + (queueWaitMs - clientTimeout) / 1000 + "s ago");
        System.out.println("  - Client already retried (adding MORE load)");
        System.out.println("  - System is processing work nobody will read");
        System.out.println("  - Dashboard says 'available' because it accepted the request");
    }
}
```

```text
Client timeout: 5000ms
Actual queue wait: 30000ms
By the time the request is processed:
  - Client timed out 25s ago
  - Client already retried (adding MORE load)
  - System is processing work nobody will read
  - Dashboard says 'available' because it accepted the request
```

### What is the memory failure mode?

Every queued item holds its payload, context, and often a thread or connection. An unbounded queue under overload accumulates these until the heap is exhausted.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class MemoryExhaustion {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int itemSizeKb = 2;  // each request payload + context
        int queueDepth = 500_000;
        long memoryMb = (long) queueDepth * itemSizeKb / 1024;

        System.out.println("Queue depth: " + queueDepth);
        System.out.println("Memory consumed by queue: " + memoryMb + " MB");
        System.out.println("GC thrashing starts, pause times spike");
        System.out.println("Eventually: OutOfMemoryError → entire service crashes");
        System.out.println("This takes down ALL work, including what the system could have completed");
    }
}
```

```text
Queue depth: 500000
Memory consumed by queue: 976 MB
GC thrashing starts, pause times spike
Eventually: OutOfMemoryError → entire service crashes
This takes down ALL work, including what the system could have completed
```

### How does a bounded queue with load shedding fix this?

When the queue is full, new work is rejected immediately with a clear 429 or 503. Queue depth is capped, so maximum latency is capped too.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class BoundedQueueExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int capacity = 100;
        int serviceRatePerSecond = 50;
        double maxLatencySeconds = (double) capacity / serviceRatePerSecond;

        BlockingQueue<Runnable> queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(capacity);
        ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
                4, 4, 0, TimeUnit.SECONDS, queue,
                new ThreadPoolExecutor.AbortPolicy()); // reject when full

        System.out.println("Queue capacity: " + capacity);
        System.out.println("Service rate: " + serviceRatePerSecond + "/sec");
        System.out.println("Max latency for accepted work: " + maxLatencySeconds + "s");

        // Submit work; once full, AbortPolicy throws RejectedExecutionException
        int accepted = 0, rejected = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
            try {
                executor.submit(() -> {
                    try { Thread.sleep(20); } catch (InterruptedException e) {}
                });
                accepted++;
            } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
                rejected++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Accepted: " + accepted + ", Rejected (shed): " + rejected);
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
Queue capacity: 100
Service rate: 50/sec
Max latency for accepted work: 2.0s
Accepted: 104, Rejected (shed): 96
```

### How do you compare unbounded vs bounded queues?

| Aspect | Unbounded Queue | Bounded Queue + Shedding |
|---|---|---|
| Overload behavior | Latency grows without limit | Excess rejected immediately |
| Memory | Grows to OOM | Capped |
| Accepted work latency | Unbounded | Capped (queue size / rate) |
| Appearance | "Available" (accepting all) | Honest (429/503 on excess) |
| Monitoring signal | None until crash | Shed rate is a clean alert |
| Useful work done | Decreasing (stale) | All accepted work is fresh |

### What is intelligent load shedding?

Shed the least valuable work first: low-priority, already-timed-out, or cosmetic requests. Protect critical paths like settlements over recommendations.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.time.Instant;

public class IntelligentShedding {

    record Request(String type, Instant deadline) {}

    static boolean shouldShed(Request req) {
        // Drop already-expired requests
        if (req.deadline().isBefore(Instant.now())) {
            System.out.println("SHED: " + req.type() + " (deadline passed, no point processing)");
            return true;
        }
        // Drop low-priority under overload
        if (req.type().equals("recommendation")) {
            System.out.println("SHED: " + req.type() + " (non-critical during overload)");
            return true;
        }
        System.out.println("ACCEPT: " + req.type() + " (critical, deadline valid)");
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        shouldShed(new Request("transfer", Instant.now().plusSeconds(5)));
        shouldShed(new Request("recommendation", Instant.now().plusSeconds(5)));
        shouldShed(new Request("transfer", Instant.now().minusSeconds(10)));
    }
}
```

```text
ACCEPT: transfer (critical, deadline valid)
SHED: recommendation (non-critical during overload)
SHED: transfer (deadline passed, no point processing)
```

### What is the Java code difference?

The literal difference in Java is one constructor argument.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;

public class QueueComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // DANGEROUS: unbounded, no capacity limit
        var unbounded = new LinkedBlockingQueue<String>();
        System.out.println("new LinkedBlockingQueue<>() → capacity: " + unbounded.remainingCapacity());

        // SAFE: bounded with explicit capacity
        var bounded = new LinkedBlockingQueue<String>(100);
        System.out.println("new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(100) → capacity: " + bounded.remainingCapacity());
    }
}
```

```text
new LinkedBlockingQueue<>() → capacity: 2147483647
new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(100) → capacity: 100
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you pair server-side shedding with client-side backoff?
- What metrics do you alert on to detect unbounded queue growth?
- How does a deadline-aware queue work in gRPC?
- When is an unbounded queue acceptable (hint: almost never for synchronous request processing)?
